Top 10 Follow-Up Questions Asked After a Microservices System Design Round

These are the most common follow-up questions asked after designing a Payment System, Fund Transfer System, E-Commerce System, Order Processing System, or Banking Transaction System.

Interviewers are evaluating whether you understand distributed systems, scalability, resiliency, fault tolerance, and consistency trade-offs rather than just drawing architecture diagrams.

1. Why Kafka Instead of REST?
What Interviewer Wants to Know

Do you understand when to use synchronous versus asynchronous communication?

REST Approach
Order Service
      ↓
Payment Service

Order Service waits for Payment Service response.
Both services are tightly coupled.
Failure of Payment Service can impact Order Service.
Problem
Payment Service Down
       ↓
Order Service Fails

Kafka Approach
Order Service
      ↓
Kafka Topic
      ↓
Payment Service

Order Service publishes an event and continues.
Payment Service processes the message later.
Producer and consumer are independent.
Advantages of Kafka
Loose Coupling
High Throughput
Better Scalability
Fault Tolerance
Event-Driven Architecture
Asynchronous Processing
Banking Example
Fund Transfer Completed
         ↓
       Kafka
         ↓
   Balance Service
   Audit Service
   Fraud Service
   Notification Service

Interview Answer

"I prefer Kafka when immediate response is not required. Kafka provides asynchronous communication, loose coupling, fault tolerance, and supports very high throughput."

2. Why Eventual Consistency?
What Interviewer Wants to Know

Can you maintain consistency without distributed ACID transactions?

Traditional Monolith
Application
      ↓
Single Database
      ↓
Single Transaction


Everything commits or rolls back together.

Microservices
Order Service       → Order DB
Payment Service     → Payment DB
Inventory Service   → Inventory DB


Each service owns its own database.

Problem

Distributed ACID transactions across multiple databases are expensive and difficult to scale.

Solution

Use asynchronous events.

Order Created
      ↓
Payment Completed
      ↓
Inventory Updated

Benefits
Better Scalability
Better Availability
Independent Services
Reduced Coupling
Banking Example
Status = PROCESSING


Later:

Status = SUCCESS


after all services complete processing.

Interview Answer

"Eventual consistency allows independent services to scale and operate autonomously while ensuring the entire system eventually reaches a consistent state."

3. Why Saga Choreography Over Orchestration?
What Interviewer Wants to Know

Do you understand distributed transaction patterns?

Saga Orchestration

Central coordinator controls workflow.

Saga Coordinator
       ↓
Order Service
       ↓
Payment Service
       ↓
Inventory Service

Advantages
Easier Debugging
Easier Monitoring
Centralized Control
Disadvantages
Single Point of Failure
Coordinator Complexity
Saga Choreography

Services communicate through events.

Order Created
      ↓
Inventory Reserved
      ↓
Payment Completed
      ↓
Notification Sent

Advantages
Loose Coupling
Better Scalability
Event-Driven Design
Disadvantages
Difficult Debugging
Harder Workflow Visibility
Banking Example

High-volume payment systems often use choreography because multiple services can consume events independently.

Interview Answer

"For large event-driven systems, I prefer choreography because it eliminates central bottlenecks and improves scalability. For highly controlled workflows, orchestration may be better."

